WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.280
Take a square tile with one corner coloured.

00:00:03.280 --> 00:00:05.190
Turn it, and the coloured corner moves.

00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:08.080
Is that a new tile, or the same tile seen

00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:09.070
from another side?

00:00:09.560 --> 00:00:11.610
The answer depends on the rules of the question.

00:00:12.280 --> 00:00:14.240
You must decide what counts as the same

00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:15.070
before you count.

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:18.470
The same problem can have two correct-looking answers,

00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:22.420
one for objects fixed in place and one for objects that

00:00:22.420 --> 00:00:23.170
can be turned.

00:00:23.740 --> 00:00:25.730
Reading which is intended is half the work.

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:29.110
A square is divided into four smaller squares,

00:00:29.480 --> 00:00:31.150
and exactly one of them is shaded.

00:00:31.640 --> 00:00:33.170
How many different patterns are there?

00:00:33.460 --> 00:00:35.860
Pause and decide whether the square is fixed to the

00:00:35.860 --> 00:00:37.770
page, or free to be turned.

00:00:41.530 --> 00:00:44.590
If the square is fixed on the page, like a window in a

00:00:44.590 --> 00:00:47.260
wall, there are four patterns: top-left,

00:00:47.730 --> 00:00:50.300
top-right, bottom-left, bottom-right.

00:00:51.140 --> 00:00:53.340
If the square is a loose tile that can be turned,

00:00:53.340 --> 00:00:55.050
all four are the same tile.

00:00:55.460 --> 00:00:58.090
A quarter turn carries each pattern to the next.

00:00:58.480 --> 00:00:59.630
There is only one pattern.

00:01:00.580 --> 00:01:02.550
Now shade two of the four squares.

00:01:02.820 --> 00:01:04.730
Fixed on the page there are six patterns.

00:01:05.380 --> 00:01:07.830
Free to turn, they fall into just two kinds:

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:10.650
the two shaded squares are side by side,

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:11.790
or they are diagonal.

00:01:12.620 --> 00:01:15.110
Turning can never change one kind into the other.

00:01:15.930 --> 00:01:18.340
Turning over is different from turning round.

00:01:18.910 --> 00:01:21.370
This marked tile cannot match its mirror by turning

00:01:21.370 --> 00:01:22.100
on the table.

00:01:22.690 --> 00:01:25.110
Flipping it over changes the order of the marks and

00:01:25.110 --> 00:01:25.860
makes a match.

00:01:26.370 --> 00:01:28.780
Some other marks do match their mirrors by turning,

00:01:29.170 --> 00:01:31.160
so test the moves that the question allows.

00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:34.910
Ask whether objects are fixed, can be turned,

00:01:35.220 --> 00:01:36.270
or can also be flipped.

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:39.680
Two arrangements are the same only if an allowed move

00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:41.110
takes one to the other.

00:01:41.740 --> 00:01:44.000
Notebook challenge: three of the four small

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:45.150
squares are shaded.

00:01:45.720 --> 00:01:47.820
How many patterns are there if the square is

00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:48.710
free to turn?
